# LIMIT with MN > MX follows the formula MIN(MAX(IN, MN), MX).
#
# Derivation (ed.3 table 32, LIMIT: "MIN(MAX(IN, MN), MX)"; ed.2 table 27
# gives the same formula; both editions define the function by it and say
# nothing that would require MN <= MX):
# cycle 0: MN=0  IN=0  MX=-16 -> MAX(0, 0) = 0, MIN(0, -16) = -16
# cycle 1: MN=5  IN=3  MX=1   -> MAX(3, 5) = 5, MIN(5, 1) = 1
# cycle 2: MN=5  IN=7  MX=1   -> MAX(7, 5) = 7, MIN(7, 1) = 1
# cycle 3: MN=5  IN=10 MX=1   -> 1 (IN above both)
# cycle 4: MN=1  IN=3  MX=5   -> 3 (the ordinary case, for contrast)
# cycle 5: MN=-2 IN=-9 MX=-5  -> MAX(-9, -2) = -2, MIN(-2, -5) = -5
# An implementation that tests MN first ("IN < MN ? MN : ...") gives MN on
# cycles 1 and 5 (5, -2) instead of MX.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: mn x mx
outputs: l
status: conformant
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 table 32 (LIMIT); ed.2 table 27
---
0 mn=0 x=0 mx=-16 => l=-16
1 mn=5 x=3 mx=1 => l=1
2 mn=5 x=7 mx=1 => l=1
3 mn=5 x=10 mx=1 => l=1
4 mn=1 x=3 mx=5 => l=3
5 mn=-2 x=-9 mx=-5 => l=-5
